  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Introduction\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU)** cache is a classic data structure that evicts the item that has not been accessed for the longest period when the cache reaches its capacity. Python's `collections.OrderedDict` provides a powerful foundation for implementing an LRU cache because it maintains insertion order and allows moving keys to the end, which aligns naturally with the \"most recently used\" semantics.\n\nThis tutorial walks through building a complete `LRUCache` class, explains the behavior of `get` and `put`, analyzes edge cases, provides unit tests, and concludes with a worked example and complexity analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Core Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU (Least Recently Used) cache using OrderedDict.\n    \n    The cache maintains a fixed maximum capacity. When the cache is full\n    and a new key is inserted, the least recently used key is evicted.\n    \n    Both get() and put() operations are O(1) in the worst case.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be a positive integer.\")\n        self._capacity = capacity\n        self._store = OrderedDict()\n    \n    def get(self, key: object) -> object:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with the given key.\n        \n        If the key exists in the cache, its position is moved to the end\n        (marking it as most recently used) and the value is returned.\n        \n        If the key does not exist, returns -1 (or None, depending on\n        the design choice; here we use -1 as a sentinel for \"not found\"\n        to distinguish from valid values).\n        \n        Time complexity: O(1)\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self._store:\n            return -1  # Sentinel for key not found\n        \n        # Move the key to the end to mark it as most recently used\n        self._store.move_to_end(key)\n        return self._store[key]\n    \n    def put(self, key: object, value: object) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update the value for the given key.\n        \n        - If the key already exists, update its value and move it to\n         ",
